L000241	\hypertarget{Appendix A}{\textit{\textcolor{blue}{Appendix A: Tutorial on Christoffel symbol in Einstein's general relativity.}--}}
L000242	The motion of a free particle in curved spacetime of general theory relativity is governed by the geodesic equation
L000243	\begin{equation}\label{Eq: Geodesic}
L000244	\ddot{x}_k + \Gamma^k_{ij} \dot{x}_i \dot{x}_j = 0,
L000245	\end{equation}
L000246	which generalizes Newton's second law to a curved spacetime, where $\ddot{x}_k$ and $\dot{x}_{i,j}$ denote the derivatives of $x_i$ with respect to proper time. The geometric properties of spacetime are encoded in the affine connection
L000247	\begin{equation}
L000248	\Gamma_{ij}^k=\frac{\partial\boldsymbol{e}_i}{\partial x_j}\cdot\boldsymbol{e}_k,
L000249	\end{equation}
L000250	which characterizes the variation of the basis vectors $\boldsymbol{e}_i$'s throughout a given coordinate system (Fig.~\ref{Fig: demo}),
L000251	where $i,j,k\in \{x_0,x_1,x_2,x_3\}$, the superscripts denote contravariant components, while the subscripts denote covariant components. The spacetime in Einstein's general theory of relativity is a torsion-free manifold. Consequently, the affine connection $\Gamma_{ij}^k$ is symmetric and is entirely determined by the metric tensor as~\cite{KPThorne73book,Wald10book}
L000252	\begin{equation}\label{Eq: Gamma_GR}
L000253	\Gamma_{ij}^k = \frac{1}{2}g^{kl}(\partial_i g_{lj} + \partial_j g_{li} - \partial_l g_{ij}),
L000254	\end{equation}
L000255	known as the Christoffel symbol of the second kind, where $\partial_i$ represents $\partial/(\partial x_i)$, and $g_{lj}$ and $g^{kl}$ denote the covariant and contravariant metric tensor, respectively.
L000256	
L000257	Additionally, the Christoffel symbol of the first kind is defined by
L000258	\begin{equation}
L000259	\Gamma_{lij}=g_{lk}\Gamma_{ij}^k=\frac{1}{2}(\partial_i g_{lj}+\partial_j g_{li}-\partial_l g_{ij}),
L000260	\end{equation}
L000261	where all components are covariant. In quantum geometry, all quantities are typically treated as covariant. Since an additional band index $\nu$ is required, we will henceforth denote $\Gamma_\nu^{lij}$ [Eq.~\eqref{Eq: Christoffel}] as the quantum Christoffel symbol.
